 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot is the only registry update channel: it carries newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest, and arrives on approved USB media. Kiosks have no network route during normal operation, so every lookup must be served from local storage.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index. Snapshot validation, index build, activation, and lookup all belong to this node. The index is a SQLite database file in the registry's local data directory; it holds one row per registry record keyed by the normalized serial number, plus a metadata row carrying the snapshot date and schema version. Query syntax, storage paths, and the index file format are private to this node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build a new index into a staging file (`registry.next.db`) so no partially imported record is ever visible to lookup. Key the `records` table by the normalized serial number, and write the snapshot date and schema version into an index metadata row.
+
+Activate the staged index only after validation and building both succeed, by atomically renaming the staging file over the active index file (`registry.db`). Retain the previous index file until the rename completes, so a failure at any stage — signature, schema version, duplicate detection, or index build — discards the staging file and leaves the last valid snapshot active, reporting a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize the requested serial number before lookup: trim surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Because keys are stored normalized at build time, the query is a single primary-key equality check against the active index.
+
+Return the snapshot date from the index metadata in every result, including `NotFound`, so a caller always learns which snapshot answered the query.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access (HLD tenet); an HTTPS directory or a managed service would make each lookup depend on the lab network and require service timeout handling. SQLite keeps validation, index build, and query inside the registry node; the accepted cost is that each kiosk rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives. |
+| Index activation | Build a staging file, then atomically rename it over the active index | Build the index in place; delete the active index before writing the new one | The HLD requires a failed import to leave the last valid snapshot available; an atomic rename never exposes a partially built index, while in-place or delete-then-write activation can. |
+| Snapshot date storage | An index metadata row | A separate sidecar file beside the index | The date is needed on every result, including `NotFound`; keeping it in the index makes lookup and date retrieval a single local read. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether an index-build failure (for example, local disk exhaustion after validation passes) needs an error distinct from a validation rejection.
+2. Whether duplicate detection compares normalized keys or raw serial representations, and which serial is reported when a collision is found.
